            <section class="sub-intro section-rise">
                <div class="sub-inner">

                    <div class="sub-title-box">
                        <span class="sub-title-shape"></span>
                        <h3>Temple Site<br>Layout</h3>
                        <p>
                            Explore the terraced structure<br>and spatial organization<br>of Hoeamsaji Temple Site.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="sub-intro-content">
                        <div class="sub-desc">
                            <p>
                                Although only the foundation and cornerstones remain at the Hoeamsa Temple Site today, the former appearance of the temple can be estimated through excavation surveys and the "CheonbosanHoeamsaSujogi" by Mok-eun Yi Saek (1328–1396), which records the temple's appearance at the time after Naong rebuilt it in 1376.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                The Hoeamsa Temple Site was created by filling in the valley below Mt. Cheonbo and building terraced stone walls to form a flat area, where buildings were arranged across a total of eight terraces, and approximately 70 building foundations were discovered.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Arranged symmetrically along a north-south axis, the site features essential spatial elements of a Seon Buddhist temple, including religious facilities (Bogwangjeon, Seolbeopjeon), practice facilities (Bangjang, Seungdang, Jeondanrim), living facilities (Yosa, Hyangjeokjeon), and auxiliary facilities (Samun, bathroom, drum tower, horse tack).
                            </p>
                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="sub-intro section-rise">
                <div class="sub-inner">

                    <div class="sub-title-box">
                        <span class="sub-title-shape"></span>
                        <h3>Seon Temple<br>Layout</h3>
                        <p>
                            Explore how Seon practice,<br>ritual, and monastic life<br>shaped the temple layout.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="sub-intro-content">
                        <div class="sub-desc">
                            <p>
                                Seonjong is a sect of Buddhism that attains enlightenment through meditative practice, distinguishing itself from Gyojong, which emphasizes scriptures and doctrines.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                In Seonjong, core facilities are not Buddha halls or pagodas enshrining the Buddha, but rather the abbot's quarters (Bangjang), the space for the abbot (the manifested Buddha), and meditation halls and retreat halls (Jungryo); the buildings are arranged to facilitate the smooth functioning of the practice community
                            </p>

                            <div class="sub-desc-img">
